<p>The U.S. Justice Department is preparing to seek a criminal indictment of Raúl Castro, Cuba’s 94-year-old former president, in a case expected to focus on Cuba’s 1996 downing of two civilian aircraft operated by Brothers to the Rescue, a Miami-based exile and humanitarian group. Potential charges still require grand jury approval, and NBC Miami reported that it was unclear whether the case had already gone before one. Cuban forces shot down the aircraft in February 1996, killing four Cuban Americans, and prosecutors have examined possible charges tied to the Cuban military’s actions. Castro was Cuba’s defense minister at the time and later served as president before stepping down from the presidency in 2018 and from Communist Party leadership in 2021. The possible indictment was reported the same week CIA Director John Ratcliffe met Cuban officials in Havana, including Castro’s grandson, according to U.S. and Cuban officials cited by AP and NBC Miami. Several reports said the possible indictment was part of a broader Trump administration pressure campaign against Havana.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/x5KSkUe4Ylf5z88sxgLK3h7qOspOqzDsUdYYNnv_cmdWOGYIv2sZyO17jkc5jLPAfUlb79m3lRE2lbZLcIFqj8GwvKwMHVi-Syd3DtFejDrEg8ghDqYobfwNEwBoTDgyEnZX7xR2PR_B_tz7oyHE7TTG6WT7ggMY5uGynq_PVTZ1esbD-_bBloDHF-4qNwQ8VOl2r5CaL2x0XFoAILoqM8UP5fSHRiMS0VIN_-7KcHzQD8jRZT74ZFkjD5Ft8U4Fhkzh2XrjOudhPDsSFA' alt='Cuba’s former president Raul Castro attending a May Day parade in Havana.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Political impetus: Mediafax, citing AP, linked the renewed effort to calls from several Republican politicians for another investigation of the 1996 incident.</li><li>Aid condition: Secretary of State Marco Rubio renewed an €85 million aid offer on the condition that the Catholic Church, rather than Cuba’s government, distribute the assistance.</li><li>Possible timing: This Day reported, citing a U.S. Justice Department official, that the administration planned to announce criminal charges the following Wednesday.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>President Trump: Asked about reports of a possible Castro indictment, Trump said he would let the Justice Department speak on the matter. (<a href='https://www.lanacion.com.ar/estados-unidos/trump-vuelve-a-poner-el-foco-en-cuba-y-raul-castro-queda-en-la-mira-nid15052026/'>La Nación</a>)</li><li>Cuban government: NBC News said it sought comment from Cuba’s Foreign Ministry and its embassy in Washington but did not immediately receive a response. (<a href='https://www.nbcmiami.com/news/local/doj-indict-raul-castro-1996-shoot-down-brothers-to-the-rescue-plane/3809522/'>NBC Miami</a>)</li><li>Cubans on the island: Reuters reporting carried by This Day said the indictment plans increased tensions in Cuba and raised fears of force as the country faced severe fuel shortages. (<a href='https://thisday.com.ng/us-plans-to-indict-raul-castro-raise-cubans-fears-of-force/'>This Day</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/news/us-moving-to-indict-cubas-raul-castro-officials-say/'>U.S. moving to indict Cuba&#x27;s Raúl Castro, sources say</a> - cbsnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/us-plans-to-indict-cubas-raul-castro-us-doj-official-says/article70984678.ece'>U.S. plans to indict Cuba’s Raul Castro, U.S. DOJ official says</a> - thehindu.com</li><li><a href='https://english.elpais.com/international/2026-05-15/the-us-considers-indicting-former-cuban-president-raul-castro.html'>The US considers indicting former Cuban president Raúl Castro</a> - elpais.com</li><li><a href='http://www.euronews.com/2026/05/15/us-reportedly-seeks-to-indict-cubas-ex-president-raul-castro-as-energy-crisis-deepens'>US reportedly seeks to indict Cuba&#x27;s ex-president Raúl Castro as energy crisis deepens</a> - euronews.com</li><li><a href='https://en.mercopress.com/2026/05/15/washington-considers-prosecuting-raul-castro-over-1996-shootdown-of-civilian-planes'>W